{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TuSsTjF3qTdugbPpARMTsg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-572\"\n3\ttitle = \"The approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk: lanes author Drafted rows, a first-approval adjudication flips and snapshots them\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tneeds = [\"WI-571\"]\n6\tspecref = \"\"\n7\tbuildtier = \"strong\"\n8\tpriority = 5\n9\tsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n10\t+++\n11\t\n12\t## Deliverable\n13\t\n14\tThe approval act on a spine row — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded`\n15\tand the `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is now the\n16\tadjudicator's, on the serial trunk side. A worker lane authors `Drafted` rows\n17\tand amends cell text; nothing else changed about what a lane may do.\n18\t\n19\t1. **Lanes cannot approve.** The merge slot REFUSES a work branch whose spine\n20\t   delta flips a `Status`, mints a row born claiming approval, or writes the\n21\t   snapshot directory — by name, listing each row, its registry and every file\n22\t   touched — each worded by what the branch DID to it — with the remedy.\n23\t   Construction-first: `staged_approval_acts` reports the set\n24\t   `staged_spine_amendments` exempts MINUS the de-approvals (a withdrawal\n25\t   blesses nothing, and `staged_drafted_rows` raises the re-approval it owes\n26\t   instead), off one shared two-tree walk (`_spine_row_sides`), so no second\n27\t   detector entered. The judgement is\n28\t   `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal`; `integrate._approval_act_refusal`\n29\t   is the rung. Verified against the record: the reader reproduces the\n30\t   pre-ruling census exactly — the four flips at `580df781` and the\n31\t   born-`Approved` rows of WI-483, WI-500, WI-501, WI-507.\n32\t2. **A first-approval adjudication arm exists.** Trigger (a2) mints ONE\n33\t   `brief = \"first-approval\"` row per merge over the `Drafted` rows the lane\n34\t   handed over — including a status-only withdrawal into `Drafted` — on the\n35\t   rungs the dial releases; a held rung is not minted and\n36\t   surfaces to the owner as before. Those row ids are RECORDED on the minted\n37\t   row (`Adjudicates`, a new `wi_convert` column) and the brief — which\n38\t   re-derives its population live, because the row is claimed long after the\n39\t   merge — acts only on the intersection, so the act cannot widen past what the\n40\t   merge handed over. Its brief renders each row's WHOLE chain\n41\t   (`trace.spine_chain`, extracted from `reattest_model` so the judge and the\n42\t   owner's surface cannot disagree) and asks one question — approve, or return\n43\t   with findings — with the `--approves` argument of the approving commit\n44\t   derived rather than typed — rendered through\n45\t   `baseline_snapshot.format_approves`, the deterministic inverse of\n46\t   `parse_approves`, so the module that CONSUMES the `;`-joined list is the only\n47\t   one that names the separator; a batch spanning two registries cannot\n48\t   mis-parse into a second registry that is neither authorised nor copied. Its\n49\t   verdict grammar is its own. The adjudication lane is the permitted actor,\n50\t   but not a blanket exemption: the merge slot derives its approval delta once,\n51\t   joins every flip to the claimed row's `Adjudicates` scope, and admits exactly\n52\t   the snapshots of registries holding those flips. An empty scope, an unrelated\n53\t   flip, a missing anchor or a registry-widening copy refuses before merge.\n54\t   `_adjudication_lane` decides the actor off the same\n55\t   `_claimed_spec_frontmatters` read the bar directive uses, and the\n56\t   adjudication already runs ALONE\n57\t   (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), which is the concurrency guarantee the ruling\n58\t   points the act at rather than a new mechanism.\n59\t3. **The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The stale \"the flip is\n60\t   the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" — false since OI-45 (b) retired that\n61\t   tool — is replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot that reads the declared\n62\t   gate authority for the tiers shown and tells the session whether the\n63\t   re-attestation is its own act or the owner's.\n64\t4. **The doctrine says it once:** PROCESS.md §4's fixed points, linking to\n65\t   PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the ruling, its two\n66\t   reasons, the division-of-labour table, the three holding mechanisms); OI-45\n67\t   (b) narrowed by one sentence; `gate-advance`, `spine-authoring` and\n68\t   `worker.template.md` updated.\n69\t5. **Tests** in the modules' existing style: seven original merge-slot cases\n70\t   (both `_adjudication_lane` actor-classification arms among them) plus six\n71\t   scope cases at that trust boundary (permitted scoped act, empty scope,\n72\t   actor-kind-only flip, return-only batch, unanchored flip and widened\n73\t   snapshot), four at the\n74\t   reader, four at the trigger, ten at the brief, one at the\n75\t   `format_approves`/`parse_approves` round trip — including the pin that the\n76\t   brief's terminal sequence cannot stop before performing an approved row's\n77\t   flip-and-snapshot act, and the mutation-proven regression that an act cannot\n78\t   reach a row the merge did not hand it. The schema pin now covers the READ\n79\t   side's copy of the column list too, and `test_wi_loader_sync` carries the\n80\t   scope cell as the third member of its `bar`/`brief` triplet.\n81\t\n82\tTwo repairs the close itself unmasked rode this row rather than a follow-up,\n83\tbecause the bar fails where it surfaces: `wi_convert.read_specs` now walks the\n84\tfolder home through the READ side's own `spec_paths` (a tracked `README.md`\n85\tunder a status directory was a broken row to the writer and residue to the\n86\treader), and the four `test_wi_convert.py` guards that a catch-all\n87\t`ConvertError` skip had kept dark since WI-504 run again.\n88\t\n89\tDeviations, the ratchet re-stamps, and the one follow-on this row deliberately\n90\tdoes not take (narrowing the owner's approval brief to the held rungs) are in\n91\t`docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`.\n92\t\n93\t## Context\n94\t\n95\tFiled 2026-09-01 (evening supervised session) from the owner's ruling made in session (docs/log.d/2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md, compiled): approval acts on spine rows - the Status flip and the anchoring snapshot - are the adjudicator's alone, on the serial trunk side, for whole-chain context and for concurrency. A lane's merge is refused on any flip, born-Approved row or snapshot write; a first-approval adjudication arm is minted at merge with the whole chain in its brief and flips + snapshots on trunk; the amendment arm's stale mechanical-tool line is replaced by the true aftermath; the doctrine is stated once. Serialized behind the copy-scope row (both touch intake.py and baseline_snapshot.py). Read the plan's section 2 before widening.\n96\t\n97\t**Standing constraint (the ruling this row executes):** if this row authors\n98\tor amends spine rows (SR/LLR/TC), leave them `Drafted`; do NOT flip any\n99\t`Status`, and do NOT run `intake.py snapshot` or write\n100\t`docs/archive/last_approved/` on this lane — drive the new refusal and the\n101\tadjudication arm on a scaffold. The first-approval adjudication this row\n102\tships is what performs the flip and the snapshot, on trunk.\n103\t\n104\tHonoured: this lane moved no `Status` and wrote no snapshot; every arm is driven\n105\ton a scaffold repo built by the tests. It amended two rows its own code made\n106\tstale — `LLR-158`'s `code_symbol`/`Detail` (left `Approved`, the drift being the\n107\tsignal) and `IF-091`'s requestors (already `Drafted`) — which the constraint\n108\tpermits: a lane amends, an adjudicator approves. Checked against this row's own\n109\treader rather than assumed — `lane_approval_refusal` 
